/**
 * Hospital Management System — Phase 2 (Inpatient)
 * Domain JDL — v1.0.0
 *
 * Companion to hospital-phase1-outpatient.jdl. Intended for the same existing JHipster
 * Spring Boot + PostgreSQL application.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * THIS FILE CONTAINS ONLY THE NEW ENTITIES. Do not add Phase 1 entities to it.
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Phase 1's entities were generated once and then hand-tuned — Visit carries
 * @Version and a hand-written javadoc, VisitStatus.ADMITTED carries the note
 * explaining why an admitted visit is never moved to payment, and BillLineItem
 * carries a source_ref column that is not in the Phase 1 JDL at all. Regenerating
 * any of those files rewrites them from the template and silently discards that
 * work. Generating only new entities means the generator never touches them.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * AN ADDITION TO THE MODEL, NOT A STANDALONE IMPORT
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The JDL parser requires every entity named in a relationship to be declared in
 * the same document, so importing this file on its own fails with
 * "Department is not declared". The workflow that actually works, verified on the
 * Phase 2 model round:
 *
 *   jhipster export-jdl temp-phase1-model.jdl     # the 22 Phase 1 entities
 *   # Concatenate from line 30 onwards: line 29 closes the application block, and
 *   # keeping that block re-runs the app generator and rewrites pom.xml,
 *   # application.yml and README.
 *   jhipster jdl temp-combined.jdl --no-skip-server --skip-client --force
 *   # then restore every tracked file the run reports as modified, except
 *   # master.xml, CacheConfiguration.java and .yo-rc.json, and re-run the build
 *   # and the full test suite.
 *
 * Three things about that which cost real time to learn:
 *   - `--force` is required, and it is destructive. The generator regenerates all 33
 *     entities, not only the new ones: without it the run stops on a conflict prompt
 *     the first time it wants to rewrite a Phase 1 changelog, and neither answer is
 *     right. With it, 104 tracked files were rewritten — including 11 released
 *     changelogs whose checksums existing databases depend on — and were restored from
 *     git afterwards. Nothing was lost only because the work was committed first.
 *   - `jhipster entities <names>` looks like the safe alternative and is not: its
 *     relationship resolution sees only the entities named in that run, so an FK to an
 *     existing entity fails with "could not find the entity <target>" — alphabetically
 *     first, so the message names whichever entity happens to sort first. `jhipster jdl`
 *     resolves against the whole model, which is the only reason the combined file is
 *     needed at all.
 *   - Client generation is skipped deliberately. The frontend is built separately, and the
 *     client i18n loader parses its translation JSON out of the mem-fs and fails on this
 *     project; `--skip-client` avoids it and loses nothing this phase needs.
 *
 * Consequently every foreign key here is a unidirectional ManyToOne, including the
 * ones that are genuinely one-to-one (Admission.visit, and one active plan per
 * bill). Phase 1 took the same approach: Patient has no inverse collections even
 * though four entities point at it. A unidirectional ManyToOne is written as a
 * single column, and where the cardinality really is 1:1 the database enforces it
 * through a unique index added in the accompanying changelog
 * (20260925140000_added_phase2_inpatient.xml) rather than through a JPA annotation.
 * The only thing this costs is that Hibernate does not know the relationship is
 * one-to-one; nothing in the application asks it to.
 *
 * Also added by hand in that changelog rather than expressible here:
 *   - @Version on Bed (bed assignment is the race: two nurses, one bed)
 *   - unique indexes: bed(ward_id, bed_number); admission(visit_id); admission(bed_id)
 *     where the admission is still ADMITTED, so a bed holds one patient at a time while
 *     staying reusable; payment_plan(bill_id) where the plan is ACTIVE
 *   - BillStatus.PAYMENT_PLAN_AGREED and the new BillLineSourceType values
 *
 * Deliberately NOT a database constraint: "one open admission per patient". The patient is
 * reached through the visit — Admission has no patient column, so that the identity merge
 * keeps one fewer reference to carry — and a partial unique index cannot join to visit. That
 * rule therefore belongs to the admission service (phase2.md, slice 3).
 *
 * Deviations from phases/phase2.md, each deliberate:
 *   - Admission.dischargedAt is an Instant, not the spec's "dischargeDate": it has
 *     to be comparable with admittedAt to give a length of stay, and a field named
 *     "date" holding a timestamp invites exactly the confusion it is meant to avoid.
 *   - DoctorOrder.details holds the order text. The spec's table lists no payload
 *     for an order, but an INSTRUCTION has to carry its instruction and a DRUG order
 *     has to say which drug and dose; without it the row says nothing.
 *   - DoctorOrder has no drug reference yet. Phase 2 §6 is explicit that a DRUG
 *     order must not become a second prescribing path alongside Prescription, and
 *     that decision belongs to the slice that builds orders. When it is made, the
 *     link is one column and one changeset.
 *   - No AdmissionOutcome field yet (death in hospital, discharge against medical
 *     advice). Those are open client questions (phase2.md §11); adding an enum value
 *     later is code-only, so nothing is gained by guessing now.
 *   - Admission has no patient reference: the patient is reached through the visit,
 *     which keeps the identity merge from having a third reference site to move.
 *
 * IMPORTANT: JDL generates persistence/API scaffolding only — no business logic.
 */

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Global JHipster generation options — mirroring Phase 1
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------


// The exported application model lists these options per entity rather than with a
// wildcard, so the new entities have to be named explicitly.
dto BedType, Ward, Bed, WardCover, Admission, AdmissionTransfer, InpatientVitals, DoctorOrder, OrderExecution, AdHocCharge, PaymentPlan with mapstruct
service BedType, Ward, Bed, WardCover, Admission, AdmissionTransfer, InpatientVitals, DoctorOrder, OrderExecution, AdHocCharge, PaymentPlan with serviceImpl

// Operational entities that grow without bound and need server-side pagination.
paginate Admission, AdmissionTransfer, InpatientVitals, DoctorOrder, OrderExecution, AdHocCharge, PaymentPlan with pagination

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Enums
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

enum BedStatus {
  AVAILABLE,
  OCCUPIED,
  CLEANING,
  MAINTENANCE
}

enum AdmissionStatus {
  PENDING_BED,
  ADMITTED,
  DISCHARGED
}

enum DoctorOrderType {
  LAB,
  RADIOLOGY,
  DRUG,
  INSTRUCTION
}

enum DoctorOrderRecurrence {
  ONE_OFF,
  RECURRING
}

enum DoctorOrderStatus {
  ACTIVE,
  COMPLETED,
  CANCELLED
}

enum PaymentPlanStatus {
  ACTIVE,
  COMPLETED,
  DEFAULTED
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ward and bed reference data
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Bed categories are per-hospital data, not a fixed enum: ICU exists in one
 * hospital and not in the next. Super Admin managed, like Department. */
entity BedType {
  name String required unique maxlength(80),
  defaultDailyRate BigDecimal required min(0),
  active Boolean required
}

entity Ward {
  name String required unique maxlength(120),
  location String maxlength(120),
  /** A ward that no longer takes patients is deactivated, never deleted: its beds
   * and the admissions that used them have to survive in the history. */
  active Boolean required
}

entity Bed {
  bedNumber String required maxlength(32),
  /** Optional per-bed price for a premium room; otherwise the bed type's rate
   * applies. Read when the bed-day charge is raised, so a rate change is never
   * retrospective. */
  dailyRateOverride BigDecimal min(0),
  status BedStatus required
}

/** Which doctor is responsible for which ward, for a period. Without this the
 * "a doctor sees admissions on a ward they are covering" rule has no data to read. */
entity WardCover {
  coversFrom Instant required,
  coversTo Instant,
  note String maxlength(500)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The stay
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

entity Admission {
  admittedAt Instant required,
  admissionReason String required maxlength(10000),
  status AdmissionStatus required,
  dischargedAt Instant,
  dischargeNote String maxlength(10000)
}

entity AdmissionTransfer {
  transferredAt Instant required,
  reason String required maxlength(10000)
}

/** Inpatient observations. Separate from VitalSigns because that entity is unique
 * per visit — a correct shape for one triage moment and the wrong one for a
 * patient charted twice a day for a week. */
entity InpatientVitals {
  recordedAt Instant required,
  temperature BigDecimal,
  pulseRate Integer,
  systolicBp Integer,
  diastolicBp Integer,
  oxygenSaturation Integer,
  weight BigDecimal,
  height BigDecimal,
  bmi BigDecimal,
  notes String maxlength(10000)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Daily orders and their execution
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

entity DoctorOrder {
  orderedAt Instant required,
  type DoctorOrderType required,
  recurrence DoctorOrderRecurrence required,
  /** Free text such as "q4h" — deliberately not a scheduling DSL. */
  frequency String maxlength(200),
  endDate Instant,
  /** What is to be done: the instruction itself, or the drug and dose. */
  details String required maxlength(10000),
  status DoctorOrderStatus required,
  cancelledAt Instant,
  cancelReason String maxlength(10000)
}

/** One dose or one action actually carried out. The nurse's record of what
 * happened, never a schedule of what should. */
entity OrderExecution {
  executedAt Instant required,
  notes String maxlength(10000)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Inpatient money
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

entity AdHocCharge {
  description String required maxlength(500),
  amount BigDecimal required min(0),
  reason String required maxlength(10000),
  addedAt Instant required,
  voidedAt Instant,
  voidReason String maxlength(10000)
}

entity PaymentPlan {
  totalOwed BigDecimal required min(0),
  agreedAt Instant required,
  guarantorName String required maxlength(200),
  guarantorRelationship String maxlength(100),
  guarantorPhone String required maxlength(32),
  notes String maxlength(10000),
  status PaymentPlanStatus required
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relationships
//
// All unidirectional: the foreign key lives on the new entity and no Phase 1
// entity gains a field, so none of them is rewritten. See the header.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

relationship ManyToOne {
  // Reference data
  Ward{department required} to Department,
  Bed{ward required} to Ward,
  Bed{bedType required} to BedType,
  WardCover{doctor(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,
  WardCover{ward required} to Ward,
  WardCover{assignedBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,

  // Admission: one visit converts once (unique index in the changelog), and the
  // bed is null while the patient is waiting for one.
  Admission{visit required} to Visit,
  Admission{bed} to Bed,
  Admission{admittingDoctor(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,
  Admission{primaryDoctor(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,
  Admission{dischargedByDoctor(login)} to User with builtInEntity,
  Admission{dischargedByNurse(login)} to User with builtInEntity,

  AdmissionTransfer{admission required} to Admission,
  AdmissionTransfer{fromBed} to Bed,
  AdmissionTransfer{toBed required} to Bed,
  AdmissionTransfer{transferredBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,

  InpatientVitals{admission required} to Admission,
  InpatientVitals{recordedBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,

  DoctorOrder{admission required} to Admission,
  DoctorOrder{orderedBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,
  DoctorOrder{cancelledBy(login)} to User with builtInEntity,

  OrderExecution{doctorOrder required} to DoctorOrder,
  OrderExecution{executedBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,

  AdHocCharge{admission required} to Admission,
  AdHocCharge{serviceCatalogue} to HospitalService,
  AdHocCharge{addedBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,
  AdHocCharge{voidedBy(login)} to User with builtInEntity,

  PaymentPlan{bill required} to Bill,
  PaymentPlan{agreedBy(login) required} to User with builtInEntity
}
